(good)\\n- Charge: neutral (good)\\n- Rings: 4 (moderate)\\n\\nThe molecule violates multiple rules for good BBB penetration: high TPSA, low logP, high HBD and HBA. It likely does not cross the BBB via passive diffusion. It might be a substrate for efflux transporters (like P-gp) due to its polar nature and multiple hydrogen bonding groups.\\n\\nLet me also check QED (drug-likeness).\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_qed", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "QED (drug-likeness score, 0-1): 0.1443", "function_name": "get_qed", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nQED score is very low (0.14), indicating poor drug-likeness, which aligns with poor BBB penetration.\\n\\nI should also consider if this molecule might be a known drug. The structure looks familiar: it might be a tetracycline antibiotic derivative. Tetracyclines are known to have poor BBB penetration. Indeed, tetracyclines like doxycycline have limited BBB penetration but can cross inflamed meninges. The SMILES contains a chlorine substituent and multiple hydroxyl groups, similar to tetracycline derivatives.\\n\\nLet me verify by checking the exact molecular weight.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_exact_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Exact molecular weight (Daltons): 464.0986", "function_name": "get_exact_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNow, I should also consider if there are any structural alerts that might affect BBB penetration. Let\'s screen for structural alerts.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "score_structural_alerts", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "{\\n  \\"library\\": \\"all\\",\\n  \\"count\\": 11,\\n  \\"alerts\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"acyclic_C=C-O\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"beta-keto/anhydride\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Michael_acceptor_1\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Michael_acceptor_4\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Propenals\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ZINC\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"drug-likeness and unwanted functional group filters\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"acyclic C=C-O\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"beta-keto/anhydride\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Michael acceptor\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Michael acceptor\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Ketone\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_MLSMR\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_MLSMR\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Dye 2\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_MLSMR\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_MLSMR\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}", "function_name": "score_structural_alerts", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMany structural alerts, which suggests potential toxicity and unfavorable pharmacokinetics. However, BBB penetration is more specifically about permeability.\\n\\nLet me also calculate molar refractivity (MR) which relates to molecular volume.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_mr", "parameters": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Wildman\\u2013Crippen molar refractivity (MR) estimate: 110.5104", "function_name": "get_mol_mr", "arguments": {"smiles": "CN(C)[C@@H]1C(=O)/C(=C(\\\\N)O)C(=O)[C@@]2(O)C(=O)C3=C(O)c4c(O)ccc(Cl)c4[C@@H](O)[C@H]3C[C@@H]12"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMR is 110.5, which is relatively high, indicating large molecular volume.\\n\\nNow, let\'s think about the specific molecule. The SMILES resembles tetracycline antibiotics. Tetracyclines are known to have limited BBB penetration. For example, doxycycline and minocycline have some BBB penetration, but they are larger molecules. Minocycline has better BBB penetration due